    {
        "content": "<p>I edited the topic title appropriately. :) And yeah this is part of why I'm asking if this statement/proof is worth having in Mathlib. It was useful for me which is why I bother to ask, but, obviously there's a big scope missing.</p>",
        "id": 617285564,
        "sender_full_name": "Alex Meiburg",
        "timestamp": 1787080138
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm sorry for the comment that the result might exist in the Riemann Roch repo; I no longer think this is the case. I was confused by the \"original statement\" (existence of meromorphic function on compact Riemann surface sending a finite list of points to given complex numbers) on the Wikipedia page for RET. The algebraic function field version of this is not hard (weaker than \"weak approximation\", Theorem 1.3.1 on Stichtenoth (GTM 254)), but the analytic version is hard and absent from the RR repo, and the comparator challenges in the RET repo require comparing both worlds.</p>\n<p>It's not completely clear to me that the Wikipedia statement captures the essential difficulty though: in our case it's easy to construct a compact Riemann surface given branching data on the Riemann sphere, which automatically carries a meromorphic function (namely projection to the Riemann sphere), and it seems the difficulty is rather showing this function is algebraic. However both results appear in IV.11 of Farkas--Kra (GTM 71), so maybe they are in some sense equivalent.</p>",
        "id": 617294172,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1787083105
    }
]